# Performance and tests

Full detail: `docs/PERFORMANCE.md` and `docs/CONTRIBUTING.md`.

Target user: mid-range Android on 4G in an Indian city, sometimes 3G on a
train. JavaScript is the expensive thing.

## The budget, enforced by `npm run audit`

| Page | Script | Perf | A11y | BP | SEO |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `/`, `/products`, `/blog/[slug]` | ≤ 190 KB | ≥ 90 | 100 | 100 | 100 |
| `/products/[slug]` | ≤ 200 KB | ≥ 90 | 100 | 100 | 100 |

Currently 178 / 181 / 184 / 176. **Headroom is thin on purpose** — a
generous budget catches nothing.

The audit also fails on any off-origin request and on any script served by
the service worker, because a cached response has `transferSize: 0` and a
warm cache would hide a regression.

## Spending bytes well

- Server components by default; `"use client"` at the smallest leaf.
- Render content on the server, pass it as a `node` prop.
- **`next/dynamic` only helps when the component usually does *not*
  render.** Measured both ways here: it saved real bytes on
  `ReviewComposer` (behind a button) and made the product page *heavier*
  when applied to the PIN checker (renders every time). Measure with
  `npm run audit` before and after; do not reason about it.
- The cart is localStorage, not IndexedDB. IndexedDB earns its place in the
  service-worker layer and nowhere else.

## Queries and images

One round trip where one will do — `loadCatalog` is three queries for the
whole catalogue, not N+1. `LIMIT` everything that could grow. Never
`SELECT *` in application code.

`next/image` with width and height; CLS is 0 on all four audited pages and
stays there. WebP or AVIF; SVG uploads are refused.

## Service worker

`public/sw.js`, hand-written, no Workbox. Static cache-first (hashed URLs,
so stale is impossible), images stale-while-revalidate, navigations
network-first with a 3.5s timeout. **Never** `/api`, `/admin`, `/checkout`,
`/orders`, `/account`, `/track` — the Cache API outlives the tab, and a
cached order page is a privacy leak on a shared phone. GET only.

## Tests

A plain script per area — no framework. Each prints `PASS`/`FAIL` and exits
non-zero. They run against a live server and a live database, because that
is where the bugs are.

- **Write the assertion so it would fail.** A test that passes against
  broken code buys confidence that is not there.
- Clean up: every suite deletes its own rows and asserts it left nothing.
- Name assertions in English, describing the property: `check("editing a
  pack does not touch its stock", after === before)`.
- Suites are **not parallel-safe** — they share a rate limiter. If a suite
  must burn a bucket, give it its own `x-real-ip`.
- Run `test:db-down` from a warm cache, not straight after `chaos` or
  `test:admin`.

`test:admin` imports the real query modules through the `@/` resolve hook in
`scripts/alias-loader.mjs`, so it exercises the SQL the application runs
rather than SQL written in the test.
